Finding closure
The previous source detached an idle TCP socket and awaited finite off-pool I/O without retaining an actor-visible cancellation/join owner. close could return before that work and an old successful response could republish the socket and negotiated channel path.

Candidate09 registers a unique JPIPHTTPRequestOwner and captured request generation before its first worker suspension (JPIPTransport.swift:327–340). Captured existing connections are already bound to that owner's token. Fresh connects and stale-keepalive retries continue through the same cancellation token; the original per-worker limits, retry count, HTTP framing and lock-based owning-result publication remain intact.

close retires the generation and snapshots all owners before any await (647–668). It cancels each captured owner, clears retired telemetry/path and pooled socket, detaches the WebSocket owner, then awaits the captured completions. Registry entries remain until each sending task exits; duplicate closes therefore observe the same unfinished owners. No shared pool/path/telemetry/registry cleanup is performed after close's suspensions. Later requests may explicitly form a new generation; old close completion cannot mutate those owners.

An obsolete TCP success compares its captured generation before publishing receive peak, cached socket, negotiated path or successful response (385–399). Rejection closes only that returned connection. Error paths map retired work to cancellation, and sender defer removes only its own unique registry entry. These checks close both the fresh-connection and detached-keepalive variants identified by the source finding.

Owner and join reasoning
JPIPHTTPRequestOwner guards start/cancel state with a Mutex. Cancellation before beginWorker makes subsequent startup fail and completes the never-started owner. If startup wins, completion is supplied only after the finite worker operation and the original locked Result publication. Token cancellation is separately serialised so an overlapping cancellation call cannot acknowledge before a previous cancellation's captured socket close. Completion is idempotent and broadcasts to independent waiters without consuming a response result.

The new optional JPIPBlockingWorker onCompletion hook runs after handoff.publish and before the sender continuation. This acknowledges finite I/O plus explicit result publication; it is not an OS-thread-exit join and does not free every suspended sender/result reference. It avoids requiring the JPIPTransport actor to resume a sender before close can acknowledge finite-worker completion. No universal deadline/cancellation bound for getaddrinfo or other opaque OS work is claimed.

Deterministic test assessment (source only)
CodexR3TransportCloseTests declares four tests, with one parameterised over fresh, reused and stale-idle-retry modes (six planned cases total). The held-response tests obtain peer readiness before closing, hold the dedicated worker's completion acknowledgement, and assert two closes have not finished while one owner is registered and unfinished. They release that gate, require CancellationError without Task.cancel, and join peer EOF. The retry case requires two accepted connections. The pre-start test proves a joined, cancelled registration cannot later open a socket. The old-success/old-close test holds publication and close around a fresh request, then checks fresh channel path and connection reuse survive obsolete cleanup.

The added publicClientCloseCancelsInitialChannelCreationWithoutTaskCancellation test covers exactly a first cnew already received and held by the peer. JPIPClient.close routes to transport.close, the initial creation task must throw, active session must stay nil, and peer closure is joined. It does not cover every JPIPClient/createSession suspension before transport registration or after a transport response has already returned. No new client-generation state machine was added. This test must not be described as complete facade or embedded-server lifecycle qualification.

The cooperative TransportLifecycleProbe releases awaiting barriers on tracked request failure. The HTTPCompletionGate blocks only the dedicated finite I/O worker, not a cooperative Swift worker. Test readiness uses explicit barriers; existing 2-second request deadlines and runtime concurrency remain unchanged. Existing worker reuse/retry/task-cancellation regressions remain separate mandatory coverage.

